# Reader on feeds

## Why

The feed and the reader were two renderings of the same archive, produced by two
extraction passes over the same source, published to two places, on two triggers.
Only the feed's trigger was on a schedule, so an `mp` article could sit in the
feed for ten hours while the reader waited for its bundle to be rebuilt.

Nothing in the reader needed a private format. The feed already carries the full
extracted body, a stable guid, both dates and the source's `original_url`. So the
bundle goes and the reader becomes an ordinary feed reader: OPML for the
subscription list, RSS for the content.

## What was decided

**The OPML is the whole subscription list, shipped in the repo.** Authoritative
on every load, so there is no seed-versus-live problem, no local subscription
store, no add-feed UI, and no feed fetched that the file did not name. Cost is a
deploy to add a publisher — the right trade, since publishers change rarely and
articles change constantly.

**Accept the feed window rather than raising the limits.** 26 feeds carry ~280
items against the bundle's 387. A fresh device starts at the window; an item
ageing out of a feed is deliberately *not* treated as a deletion, so a device's
local archive grows past the feeds over time. Only an outline leaving the OPML
removes anything.

**Dropped the quality notice and the `q` chip.** The feed carries no quality
signal and it could only have been re-derived by guessing from body length. An
ordinary feed reader has no such concept.

**Feed slugs are the publisher id with hyphens.** All 27 publishers resolve
except `mp`, which is crawl configuration rather than a publisher and has no feed
of its own. The OPML was generated once from each feed's own `<channel><title>`.

## Two things found by measuring rather than assuming

**The zhizhu-home side was already done.** CORS, the 204 preflight and working
`304`s were all live on the Worker. The plan's "hard blocker" had been shipped,
so this commit touches only the reader.

**Cloudflare's weak-ETag bug would have silently defeated the whole point.** It
weakens an ETag whenever it gzips — always, for a browser — turning `"98aa…"`
into `W/"98aa…"`, but compares `If-None-Match` by exact string rather than the
weak comparison RFC 9110 requires. Echoing back the tag it just sent gets a `200`
with the full body. The first warm-sync measurement was 26 × 200; stripping the
`W/` prefix before sending made it 26 × 304. This is Cloudflare's compression
layer, not the Worker, so it could not have been fixed on the zhizhu side.

## Gotchas

**The `entries` store had to stay body-free.** Boot reads every entry to draw the
timeline, so bodies went into their own store rather than onto the record — else
cold boot deserializes ~2 MB of HTML to render a list of titles.

**A latent service-worker trap.** The old fetch handler sent *every* cross-origin
GET to a cache-first strategy. Left alone it would have pinned the first copy of
every feed forever and the reader would never have seen another article. The
catch-all is now guarded on `destination === 'image'`.

**The DB migration is one-way.** v1 → v2 clears `entries` and `meta` and keeps
`flags`; read and star survive because a feed's guid is the id the bundle used.
But IndexedDB refuses to open at a lower version, so rolling this deploy back
would leave already-upgraded readers with a broken app until they clear site
data.

**52 round trips warm, not 26.** `If-None-Match` is not CORS-safelisted, so each
conditional GET is preflighted, and the Fetch spec forces cache mode `no-store`
for any author-set `If-None-Match`, which makes Chrome skip its preflight cache.
Both halves are empty responses — latency, not payload.

## Verified end to end

Driven in headless Chrome over CDP against the live feeds: cold load 26 feeds /
280 articles / 974 KB; warm sync 26 × `304`; offline timeline and article bodies
from IndexedDB; `mp` "Open original" resolving to the archived capture;
outline removal dropping 280 → 276 with bodies cleaned up alongside entries; and
a seeded v1 database upgrading with both flags intact and an unread badge of
279/280.

